format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

objective u1-isotope-definition

statement:
    Know that atoms of one element have the same protons but can have different neutrons, and that these are called isotopes.

misconception: Atoms of one element are all identical.

item u1-l4-choice-pair
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Atom X has 17 protons and 18 neutrons. Atom Y has 17 protons and 20 neutrons. What are X and Y?

options:
    {"id":"iso","label":"Isotopes of the same element"}
    {"id":"diff","label":"Two different elements","note":"They have the same number of protons, so they are the same element."}
    {"id":"same","label":"Exactly the same kind of atom","note":"Their neutrons are different, so they are different isotopes."}

answer: iso

explanation:
    Same protons (17) means the same element. Different neutrons (18 and 20) mean different isotopes.

item u1-l4-choice-share
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radioactive-decay

prompt:
    What do isotopes of the same element share?

options:
    {"id":"protons","label":"The same number of protons"}
    {"id":"neutrons","label":"The same number of neutrons","note":"Different neutrons are exactly what makes them different isotopes."}
    {"id":"nothing","label":"Nothing: they are different elements","note":"Isotopes of one element are the same element."}

answer: protons

explanation:
    Isotopes of an element all have the same number of protons, which is why they are the same element. They have different numbers of neutrons.

item u1-l4-predict-carbon-mass
type: predict
surfaces: lesson

prompt:
    Two atoms are both carbon. Can one be heavier than the other?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"yes","label":"Yes, they can have different weights"}
    {"id":"no","label":"No, all carbon atoms are identical"}

answer: yes

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. Build a heavier carbon atom in the next card.

item u1-l4-sort-carbon
type: sort
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Carbon always has 6 protons. Which of these atoms are carbon? Sort each one.

buckets:
    {"id":"carbon","label":"Carbon","icon":"AqCarbon"}
    {"id":"other","label":"Not carbon","icon":"AqCarbonOff"}

items:
    {"id":"a","label":"6 protons, 6 neutrons","bucket":"carbon"}
    {"id":"b","label":"6 protons, 8 neutrons","bucket":"carbon"}
    {"id":"c","label":"7 protons, 7 neutrons","bucket":"other"}
    {"id":"d","label":"8 protons, 8 neutrons","bucket":"other"}

explanation:
    Only the protons decide the element. Any atom with 6 protons is carbon, whatever its neutrons. The two carbon atoms with different neutrons are isotopes of carbon.

item u1-l4-sort-pairs
type: sort
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    Each card shows two atoms. Are they isotopes of one element, or different elements?

figure: isotope-rule-banner

buckets:
    {"id":"iso","label":"Isotopes of one element","icon":"AqSameElement"}
    {"id":"diff","label":"Different elements","icon":"AqDiffElement"}

items:
    {"id":"a","label":"6 protons, 6 neutrons and 6 protons, 8 neutrons","bucket":"iso"}
    {"id":"b","label":"6 protons, 6 neutrons and 7 protons, 6 neutrons","bucket":"diff"}
    {"id":"c","label":"1 proton, 0 neutrons and 1 proton, 1 neutron","bucket":"iso"}
    {"id":"d","label":"8 protons, 8 neutrons and 7 protons, 8 neutrons","bucket":"diff"}

explanation:
    Check the protons first. Same protons means the same element, so different neutrons make isotopes. Different protons means different elements.

item u1-x-isotope-neutrons
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

statement:
    Isotopes of one element have different numbers of neutrons.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. That is what makes them different isotopes of the same element.

item u1-x-isotopes-of
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

prompt:
    What are carbon-12 and carbon-14?

options:
    {"id":"iso","label":"Isotopes of the same element"}
    {"id":"diff","label":"Two different elements","note":"Both have 6 protons, so both are carbon."}

answer: iso

explanation:
    They are isotopes of carbon: the same number of protons, different numbers of neutrons.
